        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.027,
            0.837,
            0.043
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 8 — Nature of Matter: Elements, Compounds, and Mixtures"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.922,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": "121"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.077,
            0.746,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "In science, however, a pure substance is the one that has no other substance present in it. For a scientist, even these products that look pure can be considered **impure** if they are made of more than one substance."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.153,
            0.744,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": "A pure **substance** is a kind of matter that cannot be separated into other kinds of matter by any physical process. When a scientist says that something is pure, it means that the substance consists of the same type of particles."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.238,
            0.297,
            0.259
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.271,
            0.727,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": "According to science, how would you **classify** milk, packed fruit juice, baking soda, sugar, and soil — as mixtures or pure substances?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.738,
            0.21,
            0.915,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.347,
            0.729,
            0.372
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.3 What Are the Types of Pure Substances?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.384,
            0.744,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "Recall the different states of water that you studied in Curiosity, Grade 6. What happened when water was cooled or heated? We observed that on cooling water gets converted into ice, and on boiling it gets converted into vapour. We can get back water up on heating ice or cooling water vapour. It shows that during these processes, the particles of water remain the same. Now, let us **perform** another activity in which we pass electricity through water and observe its effect."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.548,
            0.537,
            0.597
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 8.3: Let us experiment (Demonstration activity)"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.614,
            0.272,
            0.635
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Safety first"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.645,
            0.542,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "This activity must be performed under the supervision of the teacher. Be careful while handling sulfuric acid. Do not use lithium-ion battery."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.547,
            0.643,
            0.644,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.678,
            0.572,
            0.957,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.731,
            0.665,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": "z Collect two small test tubes, a beaker or a glass tumbler, and a 9 V battery."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.7,
            0.724,
            0.946,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": "(a) Test tubes filled with water"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.77,
            0.719,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": "Fill 2/3rd of the beaker with water and add a few drops of dilute sulfuric acid to it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.81,
            0.718,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "Fill both the small test tubes completely with water taken from the beaker (Fig. 8.8a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.85,
            0.617,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": "Place a 9 V battery inside the beaker (Fig. 8.8b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.87,
            0.718,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": "Without spilling the water, carefully place the water-filled test tubes on each of the terminals of the battery (Fig. 8.8c)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
